+## ZK-FIX/C — current workflow lifecycle recovery
+
+The existing Session entries retain background launch/control/terminal evidence. The existing
+workflow journal retains current run state and parent Session identity. Cold materialization
+reads both through the public Host wrapper; no second store or inference replay is allowed.
+
+```text
+Session Completed(old) → Session Started(resume) + same-Session journal non-terminal
+                                         ↓
+                           public Host backgroundWorks running
+Session Started(stale) → newer Completed → terminal remains authoritative
+missing / foreign / terminal journal → Started never fabricates running
+```
+
+A taskId can be resumed; it is not a permanent terminal tombstone. Compare the existing
+Session event timestamps (finite entry.data.eventTimestamp for persisted emission) before applying terminal
+protection. Proven same-owner sequence order precedes wall-clock time; uncertain persisted
+ties retain terminal authority. A payload's original startedAt is display metadata, not the
+resume event's emission time. Old control updates cannot attach to a newer lifecycle.
+
+The V4 public subscribe path must forward the original workflowJournalStore reader, preserving
+its this binding and parentSessionId filter. Existing Completed, Stop and orphan semantics
+remain intact. Proof requires fresh SQLite reopen, public Host subscription, stale-launch and
+foreign/missing/terminal-journal cases, and zero inference/file effects. This is corrective
+wiring/source evidence, not Ready/usable or visual acceptance.
+
+Persistence amendment approved for this corrective slice: Started/Updated/Completed writers
+store optional finite numeric `eventTimestamp` in their existing entry.data, copied from the
+original Session event timestamp (writer time only if invalid/missing). SQL schema, entry types,
+keys and old bytes do not change. The reader validates finiteness and falls back for legacy data
+to original time.created. time.updated is an I/O clock, not proof of a new emission. SQLite upsert preserves the original
+time.created, so it cannot establish the latest same-ID resume. Synthesized seq=0 carries no
+common runtime epoch; equal/uncertain cold times retain terminal authority. Old terminal and
+control emission times cannot override a newer Started even if read/appended later.
+
+Legacy timing correction: real SQLite old writers preserve original created and stamp updated
+with Date.now at I/O time. A delayed old Started can therefore be saved after a known terminal
+even with no new launch. Missing/terminal journal already suppresses it; a non-terminal journal
+row alone cannot prove that this old Started represents a resume. Without finite eventTimestamp,
+retain the original created time and terminal protection. New stamped resume evidence still
+wins when later; legacy rows remain untouched and unknown lifecycle order is conservative.
+
+### Clock rollback and sequence namespace (ZK-FIX/C)
+
+Original AgentRuntime owns a private readonly UUID `backgroundTaskSequenceEpoch`, generated
+once per instance. It identifies only that instance's original sequence namespace, not another
+Session, journal or state owner. The existing event writer passes it to Started/Updated/Completed
+persistence. Optional internal entry.data.eventEpoch accompanies the existing sequenceNumber;
+no duplicate sequence field or SQL schema is added. A non-empty valid UUID and positive safe
+integer sequence are required before cold comparison can use that namespace.
+
+Within the same proven epoch, sequence order precedes wall-clock time: Completed seq1/time2000
+followed by Resume Started seq2/time1000 is a newer launch. Current raw live events from one
+getEvents source likewise retain their positive sequence order. Restored synthetic events keep
+public seq0; a private Core WeakMap associates them with validated original epoch/sequence,
+and Bootstrap calls the same Core comparator. No public wire DTO is changed.
+
+Different/missing epochs never make persisted positive sequences globally comparable. Their
+recorded emission time and conservative terminal fallback remain; legacy I/O update time is
+still not emission evidence. Trace IDs are not used as runtime identity. Existing entries are
+not rewritten. Tests require the actual Runtime writer, SQLite reopen and public Host plus
+cross-epoch resets, missing metadata and zero inference/effects.
